How to understand Processing times:

There are 3 json files related to the processing times:

processing_models_full_dur.json
processing_models_proc.json
wait_reference.json

These files contain as they are named the full duration of an activity, the processing time of an activity
and the waiting times respectively.

But the internal logic differs for different kind of activities (W_ and A/O_ activities):

W_ Activities:
    - For theses activities there exists a real processing time.
    - In the processing_models_proc.json saves therefor the real active working time of W_ activities
        in form of a fitted model, considering that there are waiting times between the activities
    - The processing_models_full_dur.json contains the full duration model fitted by the first timestamp of
        an activity until the last timestamp of that same activity (with an other lifecycle)
    - The waiting times in wait_reference.json contain an empirical sample of the waiting times between the
        actual working times of the processing times (they are not fitted onto a model as they appear random
        due to influence of other factors in the application process)
      -> t(total) = t(proc) + t(wait)


A/O_ Activities:
    - These Activities do not have a real processing time
    - Therefor they do not have an entry in processing_models_proc.json
    - The durations saved in processing_models_full_dur.json and wait_reference.json use as artificial delay
        to simulate the reality, because the system does also need minimal (but not 0) time to process
    - As these activities only contain "complete" transitions, I used the minimum duration distance to the
        next activity to approximate the reality
    - The total times are therefor the same as the waiting times
    - t(total) = t(wait)
    - t(proc) = 0


Quantile Regression:
    I used quantile regression for the advanced analysis (compare in the notebook and quantile_regression.py)
    This advanced method produced very noticable better results than the basic sampling from the json files
    but they need contextual data, which we did not include in the simulation to make a noticable difference.

    This is the reason why I included basic sampling in our simulator

